We start by asking, "what can we multiply by $x+1$ to get $x^2-2x+k$ where $k$ is an integer?"

The answer is $x-3$:

$\blueD{(x+1)(x-3)=x^2-2x-3}$

Now we rewrite the numerator and split the quotient:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{x^2-2x-8}{x+1}&=\dfrac{(\blueD{x^2-2x-3})-5}{x+1}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD{x^2-2x-3}}{x+1}-\dfrac{5}{x+1}
\end{align}$

Now we use the fact that $\blueD{(x+1)(x-3)=x^2-2x-3}$ to rewrite the numerator of the first quotient and cancel common factors:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{\blueD{x^2-2x-3}}{x+1}-\dfrac{5}{x+1}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{\blueD{(x+1)}}\blueD{(x-3)}}{\cancel{x+1}}-\dfrac{5}{x+1}
\\\\
&=x-3-\dfrac{5}{x+1}
\end{align}$
